        "t": "chr",
        "v": [
          "This module classifies repository files and, for tabular data files available locally, extracts each column's type and summary statistics.",
          "#### Data Tree",
          "Where the files sit within each repository. Each file is followed by how it was classified, which study it belongs to.",
          "**Repository: https://osf.io/encod**",
          "```\n└── latin1.csv  📊 data ex1\n```",
          "#### Data Files",
          "::: {.panel-tabset}\n\n## latin1.csv\n\n\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(id = 1:4, city = c(\"Zürich\", \"Malmö\", \"Paris\", \n\"Köln\"), score = c(3L, 4L, 5L, 2L)), utf8_repaired = c(city = 3L), row.names = c(NA, \n4L), class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 10, FALSE)\n```\n\n\n:::",
          "#### Descriptives Overview\n\nWe found 0 columns with at least one missing value.",
          "::: {.panel-tabset}\n\n## latin1.csv\n\n\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(Column = c(\"city\", \"id\", \"score\"), Representation = c(\"text\", \n\"text\", \"numeric\"), Level = c(\"nominal\", \"nominal\", \"ratio\"), \n    Concept = c(\"\", \"id\", \"\"), Role = c(\"measure\", \"identifier\", \n    \"measure\"), Unit = c(\"\", \"\", \"\"), Rows = c(4L, 4L, 4L), \"% Missing\" = c(0, \n    0, 0), \"N Unique\" = c(4L, 4L, 4L), Mean = c(NA, NA, 3.5), \n    SD = c(NA, NA, 1.291), Min = c(NA, NA, 2), Max = c(NA, NA, \n    5)), row.names = c(\"city\", \"id\", \"score\"), class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 25, FALSE)\n```\n\n\n:::",
          "This module runs automated data-quality checks (outliers, miscoded missing values, empty and constant columns, SPSS filter variables, category casing, problematic column names, mixed text encodings, spreadsheet formatting) on the extracted data files.",
          "We examined 3 columns across 1 data file.",
          "#### Issues Identified",
          "1 file/column combination has at least one issue.",
          "\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(File = \"latin1.csv\", Column = \"city\", Issues = \"<span title='3 values are not valid UTF-8 (bytes from a legacy encoding such as Latin-1/Windows-1252, e.g. a mis-encoded accent or apostrophe); metacheck re-interpreted them as Latin-1 to continue. Such characters corrupt silently when the file is opened on another system. Re-save the file with UTF-8 encoding: in Excel use &#39;Save As &gt; CSV UTF-8 (Comma delimited)&#39;; in R, write.csv(..., fileEncoding = \\\"UTF-8\\\"); in SPSS, &#39;Save as type: CSV&#39; with Encoding &#39;UTF-8&#39;.'>🔣 Mixed encoding — 3 values are not valid UTF-8 (bytes from a legacy encoding such as ...<\/span>\"), row.names = c(NA, \n-1L), class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 20, FALSE)\n```\n",
          "#### Careless Responding",
          "No file could be screened for careless responding.",
          "**What this does not cover.** Only 0 of 1 data file could be screened: the rest had no detectable multi-item scale block, or too few respondents. Even in a screened file, these checks only look at multi-item rating scales — the only place where a repeated or flat answer pattern is interpretable. Single questions, open text, demographics and any item outside a detected scale block are never examined. A respondent can therefore answer most of a survey carelessly and still not be flagged here, and finding nothing is not evidence that a dataset is free of careless responding."
        ]
